[size=5][b]Faster Loading[/b][/size]

Shorter loading screens when you fast travel, respawn or enter a dungeon: about 25-30% shorter on the trips we measured, on a PC and on Steam Deck, with nothing to set up.

[size=4][b]What it changes[/b][/size]
While such a loading screen is up, the game is held back by a few limits it keeps for smooth gameplay. Nobody sees the world behind a loading screen, so the mod lifts them for that time and puts them back as soon as the screen ends:
[list]
[*][b]Parallel loading.[/b] The game loads (and unloads) the parts of the world one after another. The mod lets it work on several at once.
[*][b]Faster clean-up of the area you left.[/b] The game unloads it in tiny slices of under 1 ms per frame, to avoid stutter in gameplay. During the loading screen the mod gives it 12 ms per frame.
[*][b]No extra second at the end.[/b] Once everything is loaded, the game waits a full second of "nothing more to load" before it ends the screen. The mod waits 0.25 s.
[/list]
The game's memory clean-up at the end of a loading screen (about 1 s, at most every 20 s) is left alone on purpose: it keeps that pause out of your gameplay.

[size=4][b]Results[/b][/size]
Fast travel between the same two whispers (Mountain Gate and Marketplace), there and back, averages of 2-4 runs each:
[list]
[*][b]Test PC[/b] (Ryzen 9 5900X, RTX 4060 Ti, NVMe SSD): 11.2 s -> 8.4 s one way, 8.3 s -> 5.9 s back. Per trip 9.7 s -> 7.1 s, [b]27% shorter[/b].
[*][b]Steam Deck[/b]: 19.4 s -> 14.1 s one way, 12.7 s -> 8.8 s back. Per trip 16.1 s -> 11.4 s, [b]29% shorter[/b].
[/list]
Your times depend on your PC and on how much of the world has to be loaded: long trips gain the most. Every loading screen is logged in [i]MelonLoader/Latest.log[/i] ("[Faster_Loading] Loading screen: 5.8 s"), so you can compare with and without the mod on your PC.

[size=4][b]Settings[/b][/size]
None needed: install it and loading screens get shorter; remove it to get the game's loading back. For tinkering, each part can be tuned or switched off in [i]UserData/MelonPreferences.cfg[/i], section [FasterLoading].

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][url=https://github.com/LavaGang/MelonLoader/releases]MelonLoader[/url] 0.7.3 or newer.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Installation[/b][/size]
[list=1]
[*]Install MelonLoader into the game folder and start the game once.
[*]Extract the mod's zip into the game folder, so that [i]FasterLoading.dll[/i] ends up in the [i]Mods[/i] folder.
[/list]

[b]Steam Deck / Linux (Proton):[/b]
[list=1]
[*]Copy the files from MelonLoader's [i]MelonLoader.x64.zip[/i] (the Windows build) into the game folder, and the mod into [i]Mods[/i].
[*]In Steam, open the game's Properties > General > Launch Options and enter: [code]WINEDLLOVERRIDES="version=n,b" %command%[/code]
[*]Start the game. On the first start MelonLoader installs the .NET runtime it needs by itself; that start takes a minute or two.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Compatibility[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Tested with game build 29466 and MelonLoader 0.7.3 on Windows and Steam Deck, solo and in a 3-hour co-op session.
[*]Works in co-op, and nobody else needs the mod: only your own game's loading changes. The game simulation, your save and other players are not touched, and outside loading screens the game runs with its own values.
[*]The first load into a realm (from the main menu) is not changed.
[/list]

